   ],
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Saad's most provocative line is simple: cultures are not equal."
    },
    {
     "text": "He doesn't mean people aren't equal. He means that practices can be judged."
    },
    {
     "text": "UNICEF estimates that more than two hundred and thirty million women and girls alive today have undergone female genital mutilation. The UN has estimated around five thousand honour killings a year, and many experts believe that's an undercount."
    },
    {
     "text": "Saad says Western empathy makes people slow to condemn these practices, for fear of seeming intolerant."
    },
    {
     "text": "Here, many of his critics agree with him. Human rights groups, including many founded by women from those communities, have fought these practices for decades. The disagreement is over how far that judgement should stretch."
    }
   ]
  },
